\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Positive temporal weights for signs and phases}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The [transfer kernel conditions](fdip-007Z) have elementary
examples. For #{G=\mathbb Z_2=\{1,-1\}} and #{\beta>0}, take
#{k(u)=e^{\beta u}}. The two characters are #{1} and #{u}, and
##{k(u)=\cosh\beta+u\sinh\beta.}
Thus #{a_{\mathrm{triv}}=\cosh\beta>0} and
#{a_{\mathrm{sgn}}=\sinh\beta>0}. Both dimensions are one,
so the coefficient sum is #{e^\beta}. The function is real,
strictly positive and invariant under inversion.}

\p{For #{G=U(1)}, the
[Wilson and Villain weights](fdip-007D) give two choices:
##{\begin{aligned}
k_W(e^{i\theta})&=e^{\beta\cos\theta},
 &a_n^W&=I_n(\beta),\\
k_V(e^{i\theta})&=\sum_{\ell\in\mathbb Z}
 e^{-\beta_V(\theta-2\pi\ell)^2/2},
 &a_n^V&=\frac{e^{-n^2/(2\beta_V)}}{\sqrt{2\pi\beta_V}}.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{\beta,\beta_V>0} and #{n\in\mathbb Z}.
All coefficients are positive and summable; all characters have
dimension one. The cited calculation proves the series, continuity
and inversion symmetry. Each function is strictly positive from its
definition, so every hypothesis is met. These are unnormalized
weights; no zeroth coefficient has been divided out.}

\p{At #{\beta=0}, the sign or Wilson weight is constant and its
nontrivial Fourier coefficients vanish. The resulting convolution
kills nonconstant modes, so strict coefficient positivity cannot be
removed from the injectivity assertion in the continuum comparison
of \ref{fdip-0080}.}
